1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,894
Four ten in the afternoon.

2
00:00:01,894 --> 00:00:04,298
A tiny field hut on the Antarctic

3
00:00:04,298 --> 00:00:06,702
ice. The wind is screaming at one

4
00:00:06,702 --> 00:00:09,106
hundred fifty kilometres an hour.

5
00:00:09,106 --> 00:00:11,292
And the door just ripped open.

6
00:00:11,572 --> 00:00:12,973
Your plane just left.

7
00:00:12,973 --> 00:00:15,109
The next one is supposed to come

8
00:00:15,109 --> 00:00:15,776
in a week.

9
00:00:15,776 --> 00:00:17,912
It will not come for one hundred

10
00:00:17,912 --> 00:00:18,246
days.

11
00:00:18,526 --> 00:00:20,163
Milo is the camp cook.

12
00:00:20,163 --> 00:00:23,438
Big glasses, yellow raincoat over four other

13
00:00:23,438 --> 00:00:26,043
layers. He grabs the door with both

14
00:00:26,043 --> 00:00:26,490
hands.

15
00:00:26,770 --> 00:00:27,879
Close it! Close it!

16
00:00:27,879 --> 00:00:29,864
Every second that door is open, we

17
00:00:29,864 --> 00:00:31,673
lose the heat we worked all day

18
00:00:31,673 --> 00:00:31,907
for!

19
00:00:32,187 --> 00:00:34,678
That's Doctor Ana, a glaciologist.

20
00:00:34,678 --> 00:00:35,850
She studies ice.

21
00:00:35,850 --> 00:00:38,488
Right now, the ice is studying them.

22
00:00:38,768 --> 00:00:41,305
A storm has grounded every flight on

23
00:00:41,305 --> 00:00:44,124
the continent. The main station is three

24
00:00:44,124 --> 00:00:45,815
hundred kilometres away.

25
00:00:45,815 --> 00:00:48,493
So, can two people survive one hundred

26
00:00:48,493 --> 00:00:49,480
days out here?

27
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:51,504
First, how cold is cold?

28
00:00:51,504 --> 00:00:54,774
The lowest temperature ever measured on Earth

29
00:00:54,774 --> 00:00:56,082
was in Antarctica.

30
00:00:56,082 --> 00:00:59,280
Minus eighty nine point two degrees Celsius,

31
00:00:59,280 --> 00:01:02,477
at Vostok station, in nineteen eighty three.

32
00:01:02,757 --> 00:01:05,353
Where they are, it's around minus thirty.

33
00:01:05,353 --> 00:01:08,012
That's survivable. What makes it deadly is

34
00:01:08,012 --> 00:01:08,582
the wind.

35
00:01:08,862 --> 00:01:12,333
Australia's Antarctic program says cold air pours

36
00:01:12,333 --> 00:01:14,742
down off the high ice sheet toward

37
00:01:14,742 --> 00:01:17,646
the coast. These katabatic winds can gust

38
00:01:17,646 --> 00:01:20,267
above two hundred kilometres an hour.

39
00:01:20,547 --> 00:01:23,063
Wind strips the thin layer of warm

40
00:01:23,063 --> 00:01:25,653
air off your skin, again and again.

41
00:01:25,653 --> 00:01:28,095
The CDC says high wind makes your

42
00:01:28,095 --> 00:01:30,093
body lose heat much faster.

43
00:01:30,093 --> 00:01:31,425
That's wind chill.

44
00:01:31,705 --> 00:01:33,618
So rule one on the ice: get

45
00:01:33,618 --> 00:01:34,753
out of the wind.

46
00:01:34,753 --> 00:01:36,808
A wall, a hut, a snow trench.

47
00:01:36,808 --> 00:01:37,446
Anything.

48
00:01:37,726 --> 00:01:40,189
Day two. The storm is still raging.

49
00:01:40,189 --> 00:01:42,722
Milo shovels snow away from the door

50
00:01:42,722 --> 00:01:44,832
for an hour, and he's proud of

51
00:01:44,832 --> 00:01:45,395
himself.

52
00:01:45,675 --> 00:01:48,077
Then he sits down, and the shivering

53
00:01:48,077 --> 00:01:49,544
starts. Why? He's wet.

54
00:01:49,544 --> 00:01:50,478
Not from snow.

55
00:01:50,478 --> 00:01:51,745
From his own sweat.

56
00:01:52,025 --> 00:01:54,017
Take off a layer before you work

57
00:01:54,017 --> 00:01:55,013
hard, not after.

58
00:01:55,013 --> 00:01:57,255
If you sweat into your clothes, they

59
00:01:57,255 --> 00:01:58,687
stop trapping warm air.

60
00:01:58,967 --> 00:02:02,062
The CDC's cold weather advice is simple:

61
00:02:02,062 --> 00:02:04,074
wear layers, and stay dry.

62
00:02:04,074 --> 00:02:06,396
In the cold, wet is the enemy.

63
00:02:06,676 --> 00:02:09,135
Ana hangs his damp socks inside her

64
00:02:09,135 --> 00:02:11,524
jacket, close to her body, so they

65
00:02:11,524 --> 00:02:13,913
dry overnight. A real polar trick.

66
00:02:14,193 --> 00:02:17,418
Day three. They're surrounded by frozen water,

67
00:02:17,418 --> 00:02:18,820
and they're thirsty.

68
00:02:18,820 --> 00:02:21,134
Antarctica is basically a desert.

69
00:02:21,134 --> 00:02:22,956
The air is incredibly dry.

70
00:02:23,236 --> 00:02:25,297
Milo grabs a handful of snow and

71
00:02:25,297 --> 00:02:26,392
starts eating it.

72
00:02:26,672 --> 00:02:28,579
Don't! Your body has to burn heat

73
00:02:28,579 --> 00:02:29,330
to melt that.

74
00:02:29,330 --> 00:02:30,659
You'll only get colder.

75
00:02:30,659 --> 00:02:32,219
Melt it on the stove first.

76
00:02:32,499 --> 00:02:35,863
Survival manuals agree: eating snow lowers your

77
00:02:35,863 --> 00:02:38,799
core temperature. Melt it, then drink it,

78
00:02:38,799 --> 00:02:39,944
warm if you can.

79
00:02:40,224 --> 00:02:42,290
But melting snow eats fuel.

80
00:02:42,290 --> 00:02:44,432
A full pot of snow gives you

81
00:02:44,432 --> 00:02:46,651
only a small amount of water.

82
00:02:46,651 --> 00:02:48,717
So Ana sets a rule: fuel is

83
00:02:48,717 --> 00:02:50,400
life. Count every can.

84
00:02:50,680 --> 00:02:52,847
Day four. They count the supplies.

85
00:02:52,847 --> 00:02:55,206
Fuel for about four months if they're

86
00:02:55,206 --> 00:02:57,755
careful. Food for three, if they're very

87
00:02:57,755 --> 00:02:58,265
careful.

88
00:02:58,545 --> 00:03:00,856
And in the cold, your body burns

89
00:03:00,856 --> 00:03:02,879
a lot more food just to stay

90
00:03:02,879 --> 00:03:05,912
warm. Expedition teams often eat over four

91
00:03:05,912 --> 00:03:07,646
thousand calories a day.

92
00:03:07,926 --> 00:03:10,341
Day nine. The storm finally stops.

93
00:03:10,341 --> 00:03:12,899
The sun comes out, low and blinding,

94
00:03:12,899 --> 00:03:14,817
bouncing off endless white.

95
00:03:15,097 --> 00:03:18,221
Milo spends all afternoon outside fixing the

96
00:03:18,221 --> 00:03:21,345
radio antenna. He leaves his goggles inside.

97
00:03:21,345 --> 00:03:23,334
They're foggy, and annoying.

98
00:03:23,614 --> 00:03:25,742
Day ten. His eyes feel full of

99
00:03:25,742 --> 00:03:26,877
sand. They burn.

100
00:03:26,877 --> 00:03:28,580
He can barely open them.

101
00:03:28,860 --> 00:03:30,440
That's snow blindness.

102
00:03:30,440 --> 00:03:33,026
Snow reflects a lot of sunlight, and

103
00:03:33,026 --> 00:03:36,043
the ultraviolet light burns the surface of

104
00:03:36,043 --> 00:03:38,772
your eyes. Basically a sunburn on your

105
00:03:38,772 --> 00:03:39,419
eyeballs.

106
00:03:39,699 --> 00:03:41,713
It usually heals in a day or

107
00:03:41,713 --> 00:03:42,935
two, in the dark.

108
00:03:42,935 --> 00:03:45,237
But out here you need your eyes.

109
00:03:45,237 --> 00:03:48,258
Wear sunglasses or goggles, even on cloudy

110
00:03:48,258 --> 00:03:48,617
days.

111
00:03:48,897 --> 00:03:51,296
Day thirty. Every day the sun sets

112
00:03:51,296 --> 00:03:53,482
earlier. Every night is longer.

113
00:03:53,482 --> 00:03:55,317
Soon it won't rise at all.

114
00:03:55,597 --> 00:03:57,210
This is the polar night.

115
00:03:57,210 --> 00:03:59,293
At the South Pole, the sun sets

116
00:03:59,293 --> 00:04:01,310
once a year and stays down for

117
00:04:01,310 --> 00:04:02,452
about six months.

118
00:04:02,732 --> 00:04:05,725
Darkness messes with your sleep and your

119
00:04:05,725 --> 00:04:08,941
mood. Winter crews in Antarctica fight back

120
00:04:08,941 --> 00:04:11,335
with routine. Same wake-up time.

121
00:04:11,335 --> 00:04:12,981
Same jobs. Same meals.

122
00:04:13,261 --> 00:04:15,197
So Milo makes a schedule.

123
00:04:15,197 --> 00:04:17,366
Seven a.m., check the radio.

124
00:04:17,366 --> 00:04:19,147
Noon, shovel the vents.

125
00:04:19,147 --> 00:04:22,013
Six p.m., hot dinner, no matter what.

126
00:04:22,293 --> 00:04:24,074
Main station to field hut.

127
00:04:24,074 --> 00:04:24,896
We hear you.

128
00:04:24,896 --> 00:04:26,129
Weather is closed.

129
00:04:26,129 --> 00:04:27,978
No flights until it clears.

130
00:04:27,978 --> 00:04:29,827
Keep calling every morning.

131
00:04:30,107 --> 00:04:31,176
Day forty one.

132
00:04:31,176 --> 00:04:33,236
A new storm buries the hut.

133
00:04:33,236 --> 00:04:35,373
Snow piles up over the roof.

134
00:04:35,373 --> 00:04:38,502
Inside, the little heater runs all night.

135
00:04:38,783 --> 00:04:40,824
In the morning, both of them have

136
00:04:40,824 --> 00:04:42,061
a pounding headache.

137
00:04:42,061 --> 00:04:43,669
Milo feels sick and dizzy.

138
00:04:43,669 --> 00:04:45,216
Ana can't think straight.

139
00:04:45,496 --> 00:04:46,796
Wait. Headache. Dizzy.

140
00:04:46,796 --> 00:04:48,509
Confused. Both of us at once.

141
00:04:48,509 --> 00:04:49,690
Milo, open the door.

142
00:04:49,690 --> 00:04:49,927
Now!

143
00:04:50,207 --> 00:04:53,636
Carbon monoxide. The snow blocked the heater's

144
00:04:53,636 --> 00:04:56,021
vent. The CDC warns this gas has

145
00:04:56,021 --> 00:04:57,736
no colour and no smell.

146
00:04:57,736 --> 00:04:59,525
You can't see it coming.

147
00:04:59,805 --> 00:05:03,112
Its symptoms are headache, dizziness, weakness, an

148
00:05:03,112 --> 00:05:05,030
upset stomach, and confusion.

149
00:05:05,030 --> 00:05:07,807
If several people get them together, think

150
00:05:07,807 --> 00:05:10,056
carbon monoxide and get fresh air.

151
00:05:10,336 --> 00:05:12,291
They dig out the vent and air

152
00:05:12,291 --> 00:05:14,986
the hut. Within hours, they feel better.

153
00:05:14,986 --> 00:05:17,884
That's why Milo's schedule says: shovel the

154
00:05:17,884 --> 00:05:19,502
vents. Every single day.

155
00:05:19,782 --> 00:05:22,122
And a real-life rule: never run a

156
00:05:22,122 --> 00:05:24,817
stove, heater or generator in a closed

157
00:05:24,817 --> 00:05:27,228
space without fresh air coming in.

158
00:05:27,508 --> 00:05:28,654
Day fifty three.

159
00:05:28,654 --> 00:05:30,802
The fuel is stored in a second

160
00:05:30,802 --> 00:05:32,879
shed, just fifty metres away.

161
00:05:32,879 --> 00:05:34,526
Milo goes to get a can.

162
00:05:34,806 --> 00:05:37,120
Halfway there, the wind kicks up.

163
00:05:37,120 --> 00:05:39,575
In seconds, everything turns white.

164
00:05:39,575 --> 00:05:40,838
No ground. No sky.

165
00:05:40,838 --> 00:05:41,329
No hut.

166
00:05:41,609 --> 00:05:43,892
People have died in whiteouts a few

167
00:05:43,892 --> 00:05:45,066
steps from a door.

168
00:05:45,066 --> 00:05:47,414
You lose all sense of direction, and

169
00:05:47,414 --> 00:05:49,305
you start walking in circles.

170
00:05:49,585 --> 00:05:51,811
But Milo's hand is on the rope.

171
00:05:51,811 --> 00:05:53,751
On day one, Ana tied a rope

172
00:05:53,751 --> 00:05:55,833
line from the hut to the fuel

173
00:05:55,833 --> 00:05:56,192
shed.

174
00:05:56,472 --> 00:05:58,744
Antarctic stations really do this.

175
00:05:58,744 --> 00:06:00,816
In bad weather, you clip on, or

176
00:06:00,816 --> 00:06:02,687
keep a hand on the line, and

177
00:06:02,687 --> 00:06:03,556
never let go.

178
00:06:03,836 --> 00:06:06,185
Hand over hand, he follows it back.

179
00:06:06,185 --> 00:06:08,735
It takes twenty minutes to cover fifty

180
00:06:08,735 --> 00:06:09,205
metres.

181
00:06:09,485 --> 00:06:11,923
When he falls through the door, something

182
00:06:11,923 --> 00:06:13,885
is wrong. He's stopped shivering.

183
00:06:13,885 --> 00:06:16,145
He's mumbling. He drops his cup twice.

184
00:06:16,425 --> 00:06:18,959
The CDC lists the warning signs of

185
00:06:18,959 --> 00:06:24,102
hypothermia: shivering, exhaustion, confusion, fumbling hands, memory

186
00:06:24,102 --> 00:06:26,562
loss, slurred speech, drowsiness.

187
00:06:26,842 --> 00:06:30,360
Hypothermia means your body temperature has dropped

188
00:06:30,360 --> 00:06:32,705
below thirty five degrees Celsius.

189
00:06:32,705 --> 00:06:35,188
Your brain slows down, so the person

190
00:06:35,188 --> 00:06:37,672
often can't tell they're in trouble.

191
00:06:37,952 --> 00:06:38,833
Wet clothes off.

192
00:06:38,833 --> 00:06:40,046
Into the sleeping bag.

193
00:06:40,046 --> 00:06:41,919
Warm the middle of his body first,

194
00:06:41,919 --> 00:06:42,966
the chest and neck.

195
00:06:42,966 --> 00:06:44,233
Not his hands and feet.

196
00:06:44,513 --> 00:06:46,647
That's the CDC's advice too.

197
00:06:46,647 --> 00:06:49,238
Warm the centre of the body first.

198
00:06:49,238 --> 00:06:52,057
Dry blankets, skin to skin contact if

199
00:06:52,057 --> 00:06:55,029
needed, and warm, sweet drinks once the

200
00:06:55,029 --> 00:06:57,619
person is awake enough to swallow.

201
00:06:57,619 --> 00:06:58,458
No alcohol.

202
00:06:58,738 --> 00:07:01,136
By morning, Milo is shivering again.

203
00:07:01,136 --> 00:07:03,734
Annoying, but shivering is a good sign.

204
00:07:03,734 --> 00:07:05,467
His body is fighting back.

205
00:07:05,747 --> 00:07:08,451
But two of his fingertips look strange.

206
00:07:08,451 --> 00:07:09,699
White and greyish.

207
00:07:09,699 --> 00:07:10,670
Hard and waxy.

208
00:07:10,670 --> 00:07:11,988
He can't feel them.

209
00:07:12,268 --> 00:07:15,307
That's frostbite. The CDC says watch for

210
00:07:15,307 --> 00:07:18,270
white or grayish yellow skin that feels

211
00:07:18,270 --> 00:07:21,081
unusually firm or waxy, and numbness.

212
00:07:21,361 --> 00:07:23,145
Milo wants to rub them.

213
00:07:23,145 --> 00:07:24,231
Ana stops him.

214
00:07:24,231 --> 00:07:27,024
Rubbing frozen skin damages it more.

215
00:07:27,304 --> 00:07:30,284
Instead, she warms them in lukewarm water.

216
00:07:30,284 --> 00:07:32,483
Not hot. Numb skin can't feel a

217
00:07:32,483 --> 00:07:34,469
burn, so a stove or a heater

218
00:07:34,469 --> 00:07:36,881
can hurt him without him noticing.

219
00:07:37,161 --> 00:07:39,974
In real life, frostbite needs a doctor.

220
00:07:39,974 --> 00:07:41,488
Milo tells the radio.

221
00:07:41,488 --> 00:07:44,445
The station's doctor talks Ana through it

222
00:07:44,445 --> 00:07:45,454
every morning.

223
00:07:45,734 --> 00:07:48,573
Two weeks later, his fingers have healed.

224
00:07:48,573 --> 00:07:51,342
Lucky. From now on, mittens over gloves,

225
00:07:51,342 --> 00:07:51,827
always.

226
00:07:52,107 --> 00:07:53,708
Day seventy. Mid-winter.

227
00:07:53,708 --> 00:07:55,377
The food is getting thin.

228
00:07:55,377 --> 00:07:56,978
The fuel is getting low.

229
00:07:56,978 --> 00:07:59,314
They turn the heater down and sleep

230
00:07:59,314 --> 00:08:00,182
back to back.

231
00:08:00,462 --> 00:08:02,398
Out on the sea ice, the toughest

232
00:08:02,398 --> 00:08:04,575
animals on the continent do the same

233
00:08:04,575 --> 00:08:07,298
thing. Emperor penguins raise their chicks in

234
00:08:07,298 --> 00:08:08,568
the middle of winter.

235
00:08:08,848 --> 00:08:11,244
They huddle in thousands, and they keep

236
00:08:11,244 --> 00:08:13,578
shuffling, so the ones freezing on the

237
00:08:13,578 --> 00:08:15,667
outside get their turn in the warm

238
00:08:15,667 --> 00:08:16,097
middle.

239
00:08:16,377 --> 00:08:18,603
Fun fact Ana tells every night.

240
00:08:18,603 --> 00:08:21,691
In nineteen fifteen, the ship Endurance was

241
00:08:21,691 --> 00:08:23,055
crushed by sea ice.

242
00:08:23,055 --> 00:08:25,209
All twenty eight men survived.

243
00:08:25,489 --> 00:08:28,195
Teamwork, routine, and refusing to give up.

244
00:08:28,195 --> 00:08:29,957
That's what kept them alive.

245
00:08:30,237 --> 00:08:31,320
Day ninety six.

246
00:08:31,320 --> 00:08:33,703
On the radio, finally, good news.

247
00:08:33,983 --> 00:08:36,203
Field hut, this is main station.

248
00:08:36,203 --> 00:08:38,076
A ski plane can try for you

249
00:08:38,076 --> 00:08:40,436
on day one hundred, if the weather

250
00:08:40,436 --> 00:08:42,864
window holds. Have a runway marked.

251
00:08:43,144 --> 00:08:45,956
Winter flights in Antarctica are rare and

252
00:08:45,956 --> 00:08:48,013
risky. But they have happened.

253
00:08:48,013 --> 00:08:50,688
In twenty sixteen, planes flew into the

254
00:08:50,688 --> 00:08:53,157
South Pole in mid winter to evacuate

255
00:08:53,157 --> 00:08:54,048
sick workers.

256
00:08:54,328 --> 00:08:56,931
Milo and Ana spend three days stamping

257
00:08:56,931 --> 00:08:58,849
a flat strip in the snow and

258
00:08:58,849 --> 00:09:01,247
lining it with fuel cans and flags,

259
00:09:01,247 --> 00:09:03,028
so the pilot can see it in

260
00:09:03,028 --> 00:09:03,645
the dark.

261
00:09:03,925 --> 00:09:05,075
Day ninety nine.

262
00:09:05,075 --> 00:09:06,296
The wind is back.

263
00:09:06,296 --> 00:09:08,452
Ana watches the sky all night.

264
00:09:08,732 --> 00:09:09,835
Day one hundred.

265
00:09:09,835 --> 00:09:10,868
The wind drops.

266
00:09:10,868 --> 00:09:12,798
A faint humming in the dark.

267
00:09:13,078 --> 00:09:15,065
Lights on! Every flare we've got!

268
00:09:15,345 --> 00:09:17,486
The plane touches down on the strip

269
00:09:17,486 --> 00:09:19,443
they built with their own hands.

270
00:09:19,443 --> 00:09:20,971
Skis hissing on the snow.

271
00:09:21,251 --> 00:09:22,299
Day one hundred.

272
00:09:22,299 --> 00:09:25,049
Thinner, frostnipped, and very much alive.

273
00:09:25,329 --> 00:09:28,021
So, could you survive one hundred days

274
00:09:28,021 --> 00:09:30,853
in Antarctica? Here's what kept Milo and

275
00:09:30,853 --> 00:09:31,561
Ana alive.

276
00:09:31,841 --> 00:09:33,514
One. Get out of the wind.

277
00:09:33,514 --> 00:09:35,587
Two. Stay dry, and take a layer

278
00:09:35,587 --> 00:09:36,992
off before you sweat.

279
00:09:37,272 --> 00:09:39,420
Three. Melt snow, don't eat it.

280
00:09:39,420 --> 00:09:42,607
Four. Clear the vents, because carbon monoxide

281
00:09:42,607 --> 00:09:43,508
has no smell.

282
00:09:43,788 --> 00:09:46,068
Five. In a whiteout, never let go

283
00:09:46,068 --> 00:09:46,897
of the rope.

284
00:09:46,897 --> 00:09:49,523
Six. Warm the body's centre first, and

285
00:09:49,523 --> 00:09:50,905
never rub frostbite.

286
00:09:51,185 --> 00:09:53,838
Next time, Milo wakes up somewhere hot

287
00:09:53,838 --> 00:09:56,283
and green. One hundred days lost in

288
00:09:56,283 --> 00:09:57,540
